Round 0: ~178ms \n  - LogUp GKR: ~1033ms\n  - MLE Rounds: ~114ms\n- Openings: varies\n  - Stacked Reduction: varies\n  - WHIR: ~100ms\n- Trace Commit: ~530ms\n\nThe key insight here is: at APC 0 there's less work overall (fewer AIR instances, but they're bigger), so the GPU is better saturated per-kernel. At APC 300, there are many small AIRs that have high overhead.\n\nSo what's actually holding us back at APC 300? LogUp GKR is the dominant bottleneck at 530ms, with the body computation taking up 230ms of that. Round 0 is already pretty well optimized at 275ms, and Trace Commit has seen a 1.6x improvement from the scatter kernel. MLE Rounds and Openings have both gotten modest gains, though Stacked Reduction is already 4.15x faster than baseline. The real question is whether there's anything left to optimize in the LogUp GKR body computation. Looking at the detailed metrics, LogUp GKR sits around 520-531ms total, with GKR input evals consuming about 300ms of that, leaving roughly 220-230ms for the GKR body itself. The input eval is already well-optimized with multi-threading and pre-allocated buffers, so the main opportunity for improvement lies elsewhere.\n\nThe GKR body—which runs the iterative sumcheck protocol—hasn't been targeted for optimization yet and could be challenging to batch. Round 0 has already seen significant gains (2.4x improvement) and the descriptor array batching approach that worked for MLE rounds was attempted twice but ran into integration issues with the Rust optimizer and GPU memory constraints, though the CUDA infrastructure is in place. Trace commit has also been improved with the scatter kernel achieving 1.6x speedup, with more potential gains possible there.\n\nWHIR and MLE rounds remain largely unoptimized—WHIR probably involves polynomial commitment opening calculations, while MLE rounds saw modest gains from batched interpolation but are still kernel-execution bound. Stacked reduction has already achieved a 4.15x improvement. To hit the 2x scaling target for APC 300 STARK, I need to reduce the total by about 325ms, so I should measure the current performance baseline first to identify where the biggest wins are.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7258,"cache_read_input_tokens":35906,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7258},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5a79019c-b2e6-4f68-9275-45a371b42d97","uuid":"89c27afb-18eb-45a2-a06d-4ad2e86c720b"}
