r-AIR overhead at APC 300 is still substantial, and the scaling across phases shows the problem compounds.\n\nLooking at the phase-by-phase breakdown, Round 0 actually anti-scales (getting slower with more AIRs), LogUp GKR input shows poor scaling relative to the work reduction, and GKR body appears to scale well—but I'm realizing I don't have complete metrics for APC 0 to properly compare, so I need to dig into what the actual GKR input eval numbers are. robin report: APC 0 GKR input evals = 370-397ms\n  - So APC 0 GKR body = 1032 - 370 = 662ms\n  - APC 300 GKR body = 534 - 300 = 234ms\n  - Ratio: 662/234 = 2.83x — actually scales VERY well\n\nSo GKR body scales 2.83x (more than the 2.35x cells ratio!). Good.\n\n- MLE Rounds: 183ms vs 114ms (0.62x — **anti-scales**)\n- Trace Commit: 248ms vs 526ms (2.12x — scales well)\n- WHIR: 101ms vs 218ms (2.16x — scales well)\n- Stacked Reduction: 75ms vs 76ms (1.01x — flat)\n\nThe real bottlenecks are Round 0, MLE Rounds, and Stacked Reduction — they're either anti-scaling or flat. If I could get those three components to scale ideally, I'd save around 376ms total, which is more than enough to hit the 343ms target.\n\nFor MLE Rounds specifically, the batched kernels are already pretty efficient — only ~24 zerocheck instances and ~45 logup instances across all 16 rounds. The 183ms breaks down into ~148ms actual GPU execution and ~35ms CPU overhead. So the anti-scaling is likely from per-AIR setup costs that don't parallelize well across the larger AIR count at APC 300.\n\nSo fixing Round 0 and MLE Rounds anti-scaling would be sufficient to hit the target. The question is whether that's realistic given the per-AIR overhead constraints. 148ms of GPU compute. At APC 0, MLE Rounds is 114ms - presumably also mostly GPU compute. The ratio 114/148 = 0.77x. But APC 0 has 2.35x more cells. So per-cell, APC 300 is 2.35 × 0.77 = 1.81x more expensive per cell. This is because of per-AIR overhead in the MLE evaluation kernels - even though