at the kernel instance counts more carefully, I'm seeing that precompute_logup_combinations_kernel and precompute_lambda_combinations_kernel each have around 800 instances across all rounds, which works out to roughly 50 per round. That's nearly one invocation per AIR per round, which seems like it could be a bottleneck worth investigating.\n\nChecking the actual timing data, the precompute_logup_combinations_kernel variants are taking 24.2ms and 13.2ms respectively, while precompute_lambda_combinations_kernel takes 3.4ms. Since MLE runs single-threaded, these kernels execute serially, so that's about 40ms of GPU time just for these precompute operations per round. The fact that these aren't batched means each AIR is launching its own kernel invocation, which could be inefficient.\n\nWhen I factor in launch overhead and the 791 instances of the logup kernel, the wall time contribution balloons to around 28ms for the true variant and 17ms for the false variant, plus another 7ms for the lambda kernel. That's roughly 53ms out of the 183ms total for MLE Rounds—nearly 30% of the time spent just on these per-AIR precompute kernels. The monomial kernels are likely contributing similarly.\n\nLooking at the breakdown across all the kernel categories—precompute, monomial, batched operations, interpolation, and the various other components—they're adding up to approximately that 183ms total, which suggests I'm accounting for most of the MLE Rounds overhead.\n\nThe per-AIR kernels (precompute and monomial combined) are consuming about half of the total time at 94ms out of 183ms. If I could consolidate those into batched operations instead of running them separately for each AIR, I'd potentially recover 50-80ms. Scaling this down to APC 0 where there are fewer AIRs (99 instead of 623), the per-AIR overhead would drop proportionally to around 15ms, which aligns with the observed difference between the two configurations—the 79ms gap between 114ms and 183ms tracks pretty closely with the actual 69ms difference I'm seeing, accounting for some variance in kernel execution scaling.\n\nThis is a really promising optimization target. If I can batch the precompute_logup_combinations, precompute_lambda_combinations, and monomial kernels into descriptor-array batched launches, I'd save roughly 80ms from MLE Rounds at APC 300 with minimal impact at APC 0 since there are already so few instances there. That would give me about 80ms of net improvement to STARK excluding trace time. But I need 343ms total, so I need to find additional optimizations. Let me look at what other per-AIR kernel launches exist in the MLE rounds that could be batched together—I'm seeing hundreds of instances of precompute_logup_combinations and precompute_lambda_combinations kernels that could potentially be consolidated.\n\nLooking at the full kernel landscape across all the MLE rounds, there are nearly 3000 per-AIR kernel instances total that could be batched using descriptor arrays. The launch overhead alone—roughly 5 microseconds per kernel times 3000 instances—would save around 15ms, and with parameter upload overhead factored in, I'm looking at somewhere between 20 to 30ms of savings just from reducing launch costs. The execution improvements from better SM utilization on these tiny kernels could add more, though the gains are harder to quantify precisely.\n\nBut when I actually work through the numbers more carefully, the picture shifts. The precompute_logup_combinations_kernel dominates with 791 instances at about 30 microseconds each, and that time includes both the CPU launch overhead and the actual GPU computation. If I batch these down to just a handful of launches instead of thousands, I'd go from roughly 109ms of wall time (94ms compute plus 15ms overhead) down to about 97ms (94ms compute plus 3ms for a few batched launches). That's only about 12ms of savings, which is less impressive than the raw overhead numbers suggested. The kernel execution time is really what's dominating here, not the launch overhead. The CPU and GPU pipelines overlap significantly, so the total wall time is essentially the GPU execution time plus a small pipeline drain—batching those 791 kernels would only save around 0.3ms. The real bottleneck is GPU utilization: each small kernel launch uses only a few thread blocks, so the GPU can't pack work efficiently since each kernel is independent.\n\nCUDA does overlap sequential launches on a single stream automatically, but it's not perfect—each kernel starts a new occupancy wave with gaps between them, which matters for very small kernels. Without the actual nsight timeline data showing per-kernel gaps, I need to refocus on finding optimizations that could realistically save the ~343ms I'm targeting.\n\nLooking at the APC 300 STARK breakdown, the LogUp GKR body at ~234ms is the biggest single opportunity. The compute_round_and_revert_kernel alone takes 69.6ms, so I should dig into what's happening there and whether there's redundant work or inefficient memory access patterns I can optimize. Looking at the performance breakdown, the compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel dominates with 1370 instances but each is tiny at microsecond scale, while compute_round_and_fold_kernel shows extreme variance across the sumcheck rounds—early rounds process more data and take milliseconds, but later rounds drop to microseconds. The big kernels like bit_rev, multifold, and precompute_m are inherently GPU-saturating and difficult to optimize further, so the real bottleneck is managing the variance in the round computation kernels.\n\nRather than trying to speed up individual kernels, I'm wondering if I can overlap different phases of the protocol—like starting the trace commit for the next segment while constraints are still being computed for the current one, or interleaving round computations across different AIRs. But the protocol's sequential dependencies might prevent this, so I need to understand exactly which resul